public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failureMessages = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failureMessages;
        }

        List<Id> validContactIds = new List<Id>();
        for (Id contactId : contactIds) {
            if (contactId != null) {
                validContactIds.add(contactId);
            }
        }

        if (validContactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failureMessages;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = [SELECT Id, Phone FROM Contact WHERE Id IN :validContactIds];
        List<Contact> contactsToSave = new List<Contact>();

        for (Contact contact : contacts) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(contact.Phone)) {
                contact.Phone = contact.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                contactsToSave.add(contact);
            }
        }

        if (!contactsToSave.isEmpty()) {
            Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.update(contactsToSave, false);

            for (Database.SaveResult result : results) {
                if (!result.isSuccess() && result.getId() != null) {
                    String message = 'Unknown error';

                    List<Database.Error> errors = result.getErrors();
                    if (errors != null && errors.size() > 0) {
                        String firstMessage = errors[0].getMessage();
                        if (firstMessage != null) {
                            message = firstMessage;
                        }
                    }

                    failureMessages.put(result.getId(), message);
                }
            }
        }

        return failureMessages;
    }
}
```

I'd write this as a single `GROUP BY` aggregate query rather than four per-stage queries. Four queries is within limits, but one query is simpler to maintain (add a fifth stage and you change a string, not a loop body), uses less of the SOQL "budget," and the `GROUP BY` + `Map` pre-population pattern makes the "missing stages → 0" rule explicit rather than relying on loop logic. The behaviour is identical; the code is shorter and has one moving part.

