```java
/**
 * CasePriorityHandler
 *
 * Before-insert handler that escalates Priority to "High" for any Case
 * whose parent Account has Rating = 'Hot'.  All other Cases are left
 * untouched (they keep whatever Priority the caller supplied).
 *
 * Design notes for admins / future developers:
 *   • A single SOQL query fetches every relevant Account at once, so the
 *     logic is safe for both single-row Email-to-Case inserts AND batch
 *     ERP inserts of up to 200 Cases.  A query-inside-the-loop pattern
 *     would exceed the 100-SOQL governor limit on a 200-case batch.
 *   • The handler is stateless (all static) and has no side-effects
 *     outside the trigger context, making it trivial to unit-test and
 *     to re-use if the trigger ever needs to cover more events.
 */
public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    /**
     * Entry point called by CaseTrigger.
     *
     * @param newCases The in-memory list of Case SObjects being inserted
     *                 (Trigger.new).  Mutating Priority here applies
     *                 because the trigger fires in the before-insert phase.
     */
    public static void setPriority(List<Case> newCases) {

        // --- Guard: nothing to do on an empty list (defensive) ----------
        if (newCases == null || newCases.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // --- 1. Collect the distinct Account Ids we need to look up ----
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        // If every case is account-less, skip the query entirely.
        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // --- 2. ONE bulk-safe SOQL (max 1 query, ever) ------------------
        //    Pull only the Accounts that are actually "Hot" so the Set
        //    below is small.  With an empty accountIds set this would
        //    return zero rows, so the guard above is a micro-optimisation,
        //    not a correctness requirement.
        Set<Id> hotAccountIds = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating
             FROM Account
             WHERE Id = :accountIds
               AND Rating = 'Hot']
        ).keySet();

        // --- 3. Loop over cases and escalate Priority when needed ------
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null && hotAccountIds.contains(c.AccountId)) {
                c.Priority = 'High';
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### How to read the handler (for the admin maintaining it)

| Step | What it does | Why it's one query, not many |
|---|---|---|
| **Step 1** | Walks the incoming cases and collects any non-null `AccountId` values into a `Set<Id>`. | A `Set` de-duplicates automatically—200 cases against the same 5 accounts → 5 IDs to look up. |
| **Step 2** | Runs **one** `SELECT` to find which of those Accounts are `Hot`. | Even with 200 cases, that's still **1** SOQL. A per-row query would be **200**, tripping the 100-query limit. |
| **Step 3** | Loops over the cases once more; if the case's `AccountId` is in the Hot set, stamps `Priority = 'High'`. | Because we're in `before insert`, the change is written to the database in the same DML that's already in flight—no extra `update`. |

If you ever add more per-Account logic later, the single-query pattern extends cleanly: add a `Map<Id, Account>` in step 2 and look it up in step 3 instead of re-querying.

## Implementation notes

Salesforce forbids synchronous HTTP callouts from a trigger context (they would block the main transaction and break the governor model). The platform mechanism for "callout from a trigger" is to hand off to an **asynchronous** context: I used `@future(callout=true)` so the entire batch of accounts created in one trigger execution is pushed in a **single** POST (the ERP contract allows 1–200 customers per request, and the maximum DML batch is 200, so one request per trigger execution is always sufficient and keeps the callout count to 1 regardless of batch size).

Because the `salesforceId` must be an **18-character** Id (the REST-API / integration representation with case-checksum) while Apex `Id` literals are internally 15 characters, I build the 18-char form with a small helper before serialising.

