### Why the code looks the way it does

| Requirement | Decision | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| "make the callout **right** from the trigger" | `@future(callout=true)` invoked from the trigger | A synchronous `Http` callout **cannot** execute inside a trigger (or any synchronous Apex context triggered by DML) – the platform throws `System.CalloutException`. The Future is the minimum-latency, simplest mechanism that satisfies "fire-and-forget from the trigger." |
| One request per batch (1–200 accounts) | All records in `Trigger.new` go into **one** future call → **one** POST | A single DML in Salesforce can create at most 200 records, so one callout per trigger execution is both correct and respects the 100-callout-per-transaction limit. |
| `salesforceId` must be 18 chars | `to18CharId` helper | Apex `Id` primitives carry a **15-char** representation; the helper appends the standard 3-char case-checksum so the ERP receives the full 18-char form. |
| `country` → `null` when blank | `String.isBlank` ternary | Produces JSON `null` (not `""` or `"null"`) for empty/missing `BillingCountry`. |

