### Summary of choices / "things changed"

| Point | Rationale |
|---|---|
| `break` at 50 enqueues | Guards against `LimitException` when > 50 cases close in one transaction (e.g. the 200-record nightly API call). Without this the entire DML would roll back. |
| Re-query inside `CaseSurveyJob.execute` | The async context is a **new** transaction; the in-memory `Trigger.new` snapshot is gone. Re-reading also protects against a Case whose Contact was reassigned between commit and queue drain. |
| `with sharing` on the Queueable class | Ensures the Task insert respects the running user's / context's record-level sharing, preventing cross-Team or cross-Role leakage. |
| `Date.today().addDays(2)` vs hard-coded date | Keeps the "today" relative to the *execution* moment of the job (could be seconds to minutes after the trigger), matching the requirement wording. |
| One `enqueueJob` call per case (no batch-of-N Queueable) | Per your explicit requirement: maximum blast-radius isolation. The trade-off (50-per-tx cap) is documented above with mitigation paths. |

## Implementation Notes / Warnings

Before the code, the critical issue with the requested approach:

**Governor-limit violation at scale.** Salesforce enforces a hard limit of **10 `Messaging.sendEmail` invocations per transaction** (per `execute` call in Batch, per `execute` in Queueable, per synchronous transaction, etc.). Putting a single-`sendEmail` call *inside* the loop and iterating over up to **200** opportunities will hit that ceiling on the 11th iteration and throw a `LimitException`, killing the entire transaction. In a Batch context this means that whole `Database.executeBatch` batch of ≤200 records rolls back and is retried/aborted—exactly the opposite of the fault-isolation you want.

**Safer alternative that still isolates failures:** collect the `SingleEmailMessage` objects in a list and call `Messaging.sendEmail` in chunks of ≤ 10 (or issue a single `sendEmail(messages, false)` with all messages at once; `isSuccess()` on each `SendEmailResult` element already tells you which individual send failed, so you can still count per-message success). If you truly need per-opportunity isolation for auditing, wrap the single-email send in a `try/catch` *and* keep the batch size to ≤ 10 per `sendEmail` invocation, or break the 200-record input into sub-batches of 10 before the loop.

Everything else in the design is sound: `setTargetObjectId` + `setSaveAsActivity(false)` is correct for user-directed mail, and a plain-text body avoids template-management overhead.

Below is the class written to your spec (one `sendEmail` per iteration). It will work correctly for ≤ 10 IDs; beyond that it will `LimitException`.

```apex
// RenewalReminderService.cls
//
// ⚠ GOVERNOR-LIMIT WARNING ⚠
// Salesforce allows a maximum of 10 Messaging.sendEmail() invocations per
// transaction.  Because this method places one sendEmail() call per
// opportunity *inside* the loop, any batch larger than 10 opportunities will
// throw a LimitException on the 11th call, aborting the entire transaction.
// For the 200-per-batch scenario described in the requirement, either:
//   (a) batch the emails into groups of ≤ 10 and call sendEmail once per group, or
//   (b) build a List<SingleEmailMessage> and issue a SINGLE sendEmail(messages, false)
//       call, then inspect results[i].isSuccess() for per-email fault isolation.
// Both options keep fault isolation while staying inside the governor limit.

public with sharing class RenewalReminderService {

    /**
     * Sends a renewal-due reminder email to each Opportunity's owner.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Up to 200 Opportunity Ids collected by the weekly
     *                       scheduled job.
     * @return Number of emails that were accepted by the email gateway (i.e.
     *         SendEmailResult.isSuccess() == true).
     */
    public static Integer sendReminders(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        // ── Bulk-safe SOQL (never inside a loop) ──────────────────────────
        List<Opportunity> opps = [
            SELECT Id, Name, CloseDate, OwnerId
            FROM   Opportunity
            WHERE  Id IN :opportunityIds
            LIMIT  200
        ];

        Integer successCount = 0;

        for (Opportunity opp : opps) {
            // A Queue-owned Opportunity has a Group Id in OwnerId; we can only
            // target User records (005…) with setTargetObjectId.
            if (opp.OwnerId == null ||
                !String.valueOf(opp.OwnerId).startsWith('005')) {
                continue;
            }

            Messaging.SingleEmailMessage msg = new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage();
            msg.setTargetObjectId(opp.OwnerId);
            msg.setSaveAsActivity(false);
            msg.setSubject('Renewal due: ' + opp.Name);
            msg.setPlainTextBody(
                'Hello,

A renewal is due for opportunity "' + opp.Name + '".

  Close date : ' + opp.CloseDate + '

Please review and take any necessary action before the close date.

-- Renewal Reminder Bot'
            );

            // Per-opportunity send so a single SMTP/delivery failure does not
            // suppress the remaining emails.  ⚠ See governor-limit warning at
            // the top of this file: this pattern caps at 10 IDs per invocation.
            Messaging.SendEmailResult[] results =
                Messaging.sendEmail(new Messaging.SingleEmailMessage[] { msg }, false);

            if (results[0].isSuccess()) {
                successCount++;
            }
        }

        return successCount;
    }
}
```

### What I'd change for production

| Concern | Recommendation |
|---|---|
| 200 IDs × 1 `sendEmail` call → **LimitException** | Accumulate into a `List<SingleEmailMessage>` and issue **one** `Messaging.sendEmail(messages, false)` call (the platform limit is on *invocations*, not messages, up to the daily org allocation of ~5 000 external or ~250 internal-per-user). Iterate `results[i].isSuccess()` to count per-email successes. |
| Queue-owned Opportunities silently skipped | Decide whether to also notify the Queue's members, or escalate to the Opportunity Team / Account owner. |
| No dedup / no "already reminded this week" guard | Add a custom field (e.g. `Last_Renewal_Reminder__c` Date/DateTime) on Opportunity and skip records already reminded in the current cycle, so Monday's re-run doesn't spam owners if the job is manually re-triggered. |
| No logging / error surfacing | Wrap each send in `try/catch`, log the Exception message to a Platform Event or a custom `Email_Log__c` object so the support team can see *why* a particular email failed (inactive user, missing email address, etc.). |
| Unit-test coverage | 200-record input requires a `@TestVisible` or dependency-injection seam to avoid hitting the 10-call limit in tests too (e.g. accept the batch size as a constant). |

## Explanation of the change to your approach

You asked to load **all** contacts with a bare `[SELECT Id, AccountId, MailingCountry FROM Contact]`. I cannot ship that: with 2.3 M contacts the query would blow through the 50 000-row SOQL limit and the 24 MB heap limit on the very first MDM batch, and every subsequent 200-account transaction would do the same. Instead I keep the **one-query-per-transaction** principle but scope the WHERE clause to only the accounts whose `BillingCountry` actually changed (`WHERE AccountId IN :affectedAccountIds`). That is still a single SOQL, still no query in a loop, but it returns only the contacts under the ≤ 200 accounts that moved—typically a few hundred to a few thousand rows, well within limits.

I also add an early-return guard: if no account in the batch actually changed its `BillingCountry`, the handler exits before the SOQL, saving the round-trip entirely.

